WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:05.280
Remember when "Saturday Night Live" almost&nbsp;
reunited The Beatles? Or when Sinéad O'Connor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.720
got in trouble with the Pope? For a show&nbsp;
that's been going strong for almost 50 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.531
it goes without saying that its history is riddled&nbsp;
with all brands of controversy and intrigue.

00:00:15.531 --> 00:00:17.521
"Well isn't that special."

00:00:18.964 --> 00:00:24.000
In 1974, Johnny Carson was the biggest&nbsp;
moneymaker on television. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.960
to The New Yorker, he brought&nbsp;
NBC about $60 million a year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.640
which earned him quite a bit of leverage when&nbsp;
negotiating an extension on his contract to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:37.680
continue making "The Tonight Show." The program&nbsp;
aired weeknights at 11:30 p.m., and back then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.600
NBC filled the time slot on Saturdays with a&nbsp;
"Tonight Show" clip show called "The Best of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.820
Carson." But when Carson told NBC he'd only&nbsp;
make four new shows each week, moving "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:53.040
Best of Carson" to a weeknight, he left NBC with&nbsp;
a 90-minute hole in the Saturday night schedule.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.080
The network decided to try out a late-night&nbsp;
variety show geared to a young, hip audience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.600
To run it, executives tapped 30-year-old Canadian&nbsp;
comedy writer Lorne Michaels, best known for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.820
work on an Emmy-winning Lily Tomlin special. It&nbsp;
took Michaels a year to work out the format and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.534
find a cast of seven sketch comedians, but&nbsp;
in October 1975, the show was ready to go.

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:22.020
During its first season on TV — 1975–76 — the show&nbsp;
wasn't actually called "Saturday Night Live," as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:27.420
that title was already in use. Sportscaster&nbsp;
Howard Cosell hosted an ABC primetime variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:32.700
show with the name and, oddly enough, future "SNL"&nbsp;
star Bill Murray was one of the stock players.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.320
But when that show was canceled, NBC snapped&nbsp;
up the title, replacing the bland original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.820
"NBC's Saturday Night." That's why at the&nbsp;
beginning of each show, a performer says:

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.966
"Live from New York, it's Saturday Night!"

00:01:44.966 --> 00:01:49.920
And while the show's opening sequence now features&nbsp;
the main cast and featured players' names read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.480
loudly and excitedly by Darrell Hammond —&nbsp;
who replaced longtime announcer Don Pardo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:00.000
after his death in 2014 — the "SNL" cast members&nbsp;
weren't introduced individually during the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.820
season. The obscure comedians were instead dubbed&nbsp;
the "Not Ready for Primetime Players." But Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:11.534
Aykroyd, Chevy Chase, Jane Curtin, Gilda Radner,&nbsp;
and the others, wouldn't stay anonymous for long.

00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:16.980
Nowadays, the show is pretty much all comedy with&nbsp;
a couple of musical performances thrown in. But in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:22.320
the early years, "Saturday Night Live" was a true&nbsp;
variety show. The Muppets were frequent guests on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.580
the late-night circuit in the mid-'70s, so it&nbsp;
only made sense to include them. But we're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:32.040
talking about Kermit and the gang here. Instead,&nbsp;
Jim Henson and his cohorts created a whole slew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:37.034
of new, monstrous, adult-oriented creatures&nbsp;
for a segment called "The Land of Gorch."

00:02:37.034 --> 00:02:43.750
"And I'm going to stick my fingers in his ears,&nbsp;
and I'm going to shake him until he's dead!"

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:48.720
It might have been the first and only time&nbsp;
a Muppets project was not well-received.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.920
Considered not as fresh and urgent&nbsp;
as the "SNL" manic sketch comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:58.140
the segment ground each show to a halt. And due&nbsp;
to Writers Guild rules, the sketches couldn't be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.760
written by Muppets writers — leaving regular&nbsp;
"SNL" writers to do it, and they resented the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:07.311
puppets for taking up valuable airtime. The&nbsp;
Muppets were gone after the first season.

00:03:07.311 --> 00:03:12.000
Per Business Insider, here's the timetable&nbsp;
for how an episode of "SNL" is produced:

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.220
Monday: The guest host arrives at 30 Rockefeller&nbsp;
Center in New York City, where "SNL" is shot. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:21.000
go to Michaels' office, where they talk to&nbsp;
writers and cast members about their comedic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.200
strengths, impressions they can do, and other&nbsp;
suggestions. Writers then pitch their ideas.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:30.120
Tuesday: As a holdover from the show's&nbsp;
cocaine-fueled days in the '70s, writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:35.040
spend all day and night writing their sketches.&nbsp;
The host and a cast member also shoot short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.600
commercials to promote the episode. They're edited&nbsp;
and put on the air within hours of being shot.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.940
Wednesday: The table read is at 4 p.m. Every&nbsp;
proposed sketch is included, so it usually takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:49.500
at least three hours. Then producers and the head&nbsp;
writers determine which sketches are good enough.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:55.260
Thursday: At 6 a.m., builders begin constructing&nbsp;
sets at a shop in the Brooklyn Navy Yard. At 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.260 --> 00:04:00.900
Rock, the crew firms up plans for costumes, wigs,&nbsp;
and makeup. Any pre-taped video bits are recorded.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.140
Friday: Rehearsals are performed&nbsp;
and rewrites are hashed out.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:07.680
Saturday: The sketch order is set&nbsp;
and presented to a live audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:13.128
at 8 p.m. Any last-minute changes happen&nbsp;
by 11:30 p.m. when the show goes live.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.560
After one "SNL" season ends and another begins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:20.940
at least a couple of veteran cast members&nbsp;
decide to leave the series. That presents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:25.140
an opportunity for "SNL" brass to bring in&nbsp;
new performers, which helps keep the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:30.180
fresh in terms of comedic voices and styles.&nbsp;
"SNL" talent scouts and producers find those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.980
next-generation and emerging stars who might be&nbsp;
a good fit for the show by casting a wide net.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.780
While they'll consider online comedians and&nbsp;
take recommendations, comedy hotbeds like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:44.820
Groundlings in Los Angeles or the Upright Citizens&nbsp;
Brigade in New York often arrange showcases for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:49.920
top talent, and Michaels and other show staffers,&nbsp;
writers, and cast members attend. If they like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:54.840
what they see, they invite the performers to an&nbsp;
audition on the "SNL" stage. Hopefuls have 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:59.785
minutes to do stand-up or present at least two&nbsp;
original characters and celebrity impressions.

00:04:59.785 --> 00:05:05.256
"I'm Penelope Cruz. I'm always searching&nbsp;
for the most perfect moisturizer."

00:05:05.256 --> 00:05:09.000
With more than a dozen performers and&nbsp;
at least that many writers in direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.200
competition with each other to fill just&nbsp;
about an hour of sketch time, somebody is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.660
going to get left out. In his book "Gasping for&nbsp;
Airtime," former cast member Jay Mohr said he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:23.520
was so desperate to get a sketch on the air&nbsp;
in 1995, that he stole a routine by comedian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:29.340
Rick Shapiro and turned it into "O'Callahan &amp;&nbsp;
Son Pub." Two months later, "SNL" fired Mohr.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:34.020
And while Larry David eventually went on to&nbsp;
co-create "Seinfeld" and star in "Curb Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:39.120
Enthusiasm," he had an uneventful spell&nbsp;
as an "SNL" writer in 1984. Back then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.100
only one of his sketches made it to air: an odd&nbsp;
piece called "Going Up." In it, an architect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.700
becomes frustrated that a client wants to make&nbsp;
sure that the elevators in the new building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:53.040
have stools for elevator operators. David later&nbsp;
recycled the bit for an episode of "Seinfeld."

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.010
"Not bad!"

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:05.880
It's obvious which guest hosts "SNL" likes the&nbsp;
most, because they're the ones who keep coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.260
back. Steve Martin, Alec Baldwin, Woody&nbsp;
Harrelson, and Melissa McCarthy are a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:14.760
who have meshed well with the program. On the&nbsp;
other end of the spectrum, there are hosts who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:19.080
will likely never return because they were&nbsp;
difficult to work with, obnoxious, or both.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:23.220
In a 1992 episode, host Nicolas&nbsp;
Cage confides to Michaels that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:27.146
he fears he'll be the worst host in&nbsp;
the show's history. Michaels replies:

00:06:27.146 --> 00:06:29.126
"No, no. That would be Steven Seagal."

00:06:29.126 --> 00:06:33.000
Al Franken wrote that joke, but many&nbsp;
SNL alumni, including David Spade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.880
have named the martial arts&nbsp;
actor the show's worst host.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:40.920
But according to Michaels, the worst was&nbsp;
probably early TV star Milton Berle, who guested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:46.500
in 1979 and disrupted the show with mugging,&nbsp;
ad-libbing of old and tired jokes, and arranged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.840
beforehand to have the studio audience give him&nbsp;
a standing ovation at the end. Tina Fey, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.400
didn't beat around the bush when she told Howard&nbsp;
Stern who she thought was the worst: Paris Hilton.

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:58.604
"That's hot."

00:07:00.123 --> 00:07:03.660
While many of the show's performers&nbsp;
come from an improv comedy background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:08.280
it's forbidden to improvise on SNL. The&nbsp;
show has to be planned down to the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:11.940
to account for each sketch, musical&nbsp;
performance, and a commercial break.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:17.100
But not every host and musical guest follows&nbsp;
that rule, and many were subsequently banned.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.840
In 2003, Adrien Brody did an impromptu,&nbsp;
borderline racist bit with a Jamaican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:27.180
accent while introducing reggae singer Sean&nbsp;
Paul. In 1996, Rage Against the Machine asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:32.100
for two upside-down American flags to be hung on&nbsp;
the stage. When producers said no, the band hung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:36.720
them up anyway. Stagehands tore them down just&nbsp;
seconds before they were supposed to perform, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:41.280
their second song was canceled. The musicians were&nbsp;
escorted from the building before the episode had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.560
even finished. Sen Dog of Cypress Hill claimed his&nbsp;
group earned a ban after smoking marijuana during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.229
their "SNL" performance. And let's not forget&nbsp;
Sinead O'Connor's infamous pope incident in 1992.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:58.260
Just because an "SNL" episode airs, it doesn't&nbsp;
mean it stays that way forever. Producers often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:03.000
tinker with shows for reruns, replacing a sketch&nbsp;
that went poorly with a superior version recorded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.920
during dress rehearsal. That means some "lost"&nbsp;
sketches only survive on DVD and streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:14.100
versions. One of them, "Butt County Dance Party,"&nbsp;
aired in 1976. In the sketch, a small-town sheriff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:18.840
played by Dan Akroyd hosts a TV dance show, and&nbsp;
"winners" get to have their names run through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:23.640
a teletype machine to check for outstanding&nbsp;
warrants. But when the machine malfunctioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:27.480
Akroyd tried to improvise and told everyone&nbsp;
to keep dancing, although there was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:32.880
music. NBC cut to random stock footage of car&nbsp;
crashes, then pulled the sketch from reruns.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:38.040
In another instance from 1985, NBC president&nbsp;
Brandon Tartikoff appeared in a sketch to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:42.240
collect urine samples from cast members for&nbsp;
drug testing, but it was later deemed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:47.795
in poor taste. In the reruns, it's swapped out&nbsp;
for an extra musical performance by Simple Minds.

00:08:47.795 --> 00:08:52.800
For decades, each episode of "Saturday Night&nbsp;
Live" has basically followed the same format:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.180
a bunch of standalone sketches, a couple of&nbsp;
musical performances, and "Weekend Update"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:01.740
somewhere in the middle. In 1986, that&nbsp;
all went out the window — but just once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:06.660
to cater to a very special guest. Acclaimed&nbsp;
director Francis Ford Coppola was the host,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:10.560
and the through-line of the episode was&nbsp;
that Coppola was "directing" the show.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:15.060
The credits were stylized like those of an&nbsp;
art film and the music was provided by that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:19.980
night's musical guest, avant-garde composer,&nbsp;
Philip Glass. "Cheers" star George Wendt was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:24.120
on hand to deliver the opening monologue and&nbsp;
appeared in sketches, while Coppola didn't act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:28.680
in anything apart from appearing as himself,&nbsp;
sitting in a director's chair and interrupting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:33.000
the show every few minutes to critique the actors'&nbsp;
performances or other aspects of the production.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:38.040
"Dennis, you can't do a satellite interview&nbsp;
with Pinochet, it's too much like Ted KOH-puhl."&nbsp;

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:42.292
"KAH-puhl."
"Sorry, KAH-puhl. Anyway, it's not original."

00:09:42.292 --> 00:09:46.260
He also appeared in vignettes with Michaels&nbsp;
and cast member Terry Sweeney to discuss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:51.720
innovative ways to save the show, which NBC&nbsp;
was actually considering canceling at the time.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:56.040
Many of the most popular "SNL" characters&nbsp;
have been spun off into feature films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:59.280
but very few of them have been&nbsp;
financially successful. Some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:02.334
these include "The Blues Brothers"&nbsp;
and the classic "Wayne's World."

00:10:02.334 --> 00:10:04.434
"Does this guy know how to party, or what?"

00:10:04.434 --> 00:10:08.640
It's the $100 million-plus box office&nbsp;
take of the latter that encouraged the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:13.800
production of more and more "SNL" movies&nbsp;
in the 1990s and early 2000s. "A Night at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:18.240
the Roxbury" offered a look at the backstory&nbsp;
and home life of those unnamed guys — played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:22.800
by Will Ferrell and Chris Kattan — who bobbed&nbsp;
their heads to Haddaway's "What is Love?" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.460
tried to pick up uninterested women in&nbsp;
a series of sketches in the mid-1990s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:31.500
It earned a respectable $30 million at the&nbsp;
box office in 1999, and so did "Superstar,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:35.534
about Molly Shannon's awkward schoolgirl&nbsp;
character, Mary Katherine Gallagher.

00:10:35.534 --> 00:10:36.860
"Superstar!"

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:41.400
Other films didn't fare so well. "It's Pat,"&nbsp;
revolving around Julia Sweeney's character, Pat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:45.780
Riley — the joke being that people couldn't figure&nbsp;
out the character's gender — barely saw release in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:51.360
1994, nor did Al Franken's Stuart Smalley vehicle,&nbsp;
"Stuart Saves His Family," the following year. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:57.447
of 2023, the last "SNL"-based movie to see a&nbsp;
theatrical release was "MacGruber" in 2010.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.860
Not every single aspect of "Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live" is produced as its live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:06.120
broadcast. Since the beginning of the&nbsp;
show in the 1970s, pre-taped segments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.840
short films, and commercial parodies&nbsp;
have almost always been a part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.160
the "SNL" format. Tom Schiller produced&nbsp;
standalone films for "SNL" in its early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:18.265
years, such as the flash-forward death&nbsp;
meditation, "Don't Look Back in Anger."

00:11:18.265 --> 00:11:26.280
"They're all dead…I'll tell&nbsp;
you why…Because I'm a dancer."

00:11:26.347 --> 00:11:31.320
In the '70s, comedian and filmmaker Albert Brooks&nbsp;
rejected Michaels' offer to make him the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:36.240
permanent host and instead offered to create&nbsp;
short films. Mockumentary master Christopher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:40.680
Guest also contributed short features as&nbsp;
a cast member in the '80s. Robert Smigel's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:45.180
animated "TV Funhouse" anthology — featuring&nbsp;
"The X-Presidents," "Fun With Real Audio,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:50.760
and "The Ambiguously Gay Duo," broke up "SNL"&nbsp;
episodes in the 2000s. And The Lonely Island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:55.620
brought more than just Andy Samberg to "Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live" — they also brought comic rap videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.620 --> 00:12:01.260
and viral hits like "Lazy Sunday" and "I'm&nbsp;
on a Boat." In 2021, "SNL" brought on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.260 --> 00:12:05.695
internet comedy troupe Please Don't Destroy&nbsp;
to produce semi-weekly videos for the show.

00:12:05.935 --> 00:12:10.140
Once they've finally made it into&nbsp;
the cast of "SNL," the worrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:13.980
doesn't end for performers. They&nbsp;
can and have been abruptly fired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:18.480
from the show. Damon Wayans told The&nbsp;
Weekender of his 1985-1986 tenure:

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:21.240
"They didn't let me do what&nbsp;
I wanted to do on 'SNL.'"

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:25.020
He got so frustrated that he did&nbsp;
something he knew would lead to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:29.460
termination — he deviated from script&nbsp;
and rehearsal and in a live sketch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:33.960
portrayed a police officer character&nbsp;
as a flamboyant LGBTQ stereotype.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:38.220
In 1995, "SNL" cleaned house. In&nbsp;
the wake of a blistering New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:42.600
Magazine cover story that hailed the show's&nbsp;
demise, and as ratings subsequently fell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:47.640
Michaels fired nearly everyone — including&nbsp;
major standouts like Adam Sandler and Chris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:51.900
Farley. David Spade, Tim Meadows, and a&nbsp;
few others were spared. The next season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:56.700
Michaels brought on new hires like Will&nbsp;
Ferrell, Jim Breuer, and Cheri Oteri.

00:12:56.700 --> 00:13:02.520
And in 1998, NBC executive Dick Ebersol —&nbsp;
who helped develop "SNL" in the '70s — fired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:06.120
"Weekend Update" anchor Norm MacDonald,&nbsp;
reportedly due to his constant jokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:10.915
about accused killer O.J. Simpson; Ebersol&nbsp;
happened to be one of Simpson's friends.

00:13:10.915 --> 00:13:14.520
"She exclaimed, 'He did&nbsp;
it!'... Reached for comment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:17.820
O.J. said 'My mom was just guessing.&nbsp;
I hadn't even told her yet."

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:23.571
More recently, Taran Killam and Jay Pharaoh&nbsp;
were both let go after six years on the show.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:28.980
Only a couple hundred performers have ever&nbsp;
been full-fledged "SNL" cast members. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:33.540
an achievement unto itself for a young comedian,&nbsp;
if not a launchpad to future entertainment world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:38.040
success. But some people's time with the show&nbsp;
ended before it could even begin. During the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:43.740
1980-1981 season, Emily Prager and Laurie&nbsp;
Metcalf joined "SNL" as featured players.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:47.460
A writers' strike ended the "SNL"&nbsp;
season prematurely at 13 episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:51.960
by which point Metcalf had appeared in&nbsp;
only a few segments and Prager hadn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:56.100
appeared at all. They exited the cast&nbsp;
shortly thereafter. Emmy Award-winning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:59.460
actor Catherine O'Hara actually&nbsp;
joined "SNL" in the early '80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:05.160
during a hiatus from Canada's "SCTV," and quit&nbsp;
a couple of weeks before the "SNL" season began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:10.740
airing — although she did go on to have a turn&nbsp;
as a host in 1992. O'Hara told the Toronto Star:

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:12.360
"I hung out with some nice people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.660
tried to come up with some ideas,&nbsp;
but I never really felt involved."

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:21.360
In September 2019, "SNL" announced that comedian&nbsp;
and podcaster Shane Gillis had landed a spot in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:24.900
the cast. Within the week, footage of&nbsp;
Gillis uttering slurs on his podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:29.040
offensive to people of Chinese descent&nbsp;
and to members of the LGBTQ community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:35.304
surfaced online and went viral. Days later, before&nbsp;
ever appearing on the show, Gillis was fired.

00:14:35.534 --> 00:14:40.860
According to the New York Post, six years after&nbsp;
the Beatles split up in 1970, concert promoter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:48.000
Sid Bernstein offered the Fab Four a whopping $230&nbsp;
million to get back together and go on tour. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:52.380
band members declined, but the cultural incident&nbsp;
was fresh in the minds of "Saturday Night Live"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:57.660
viewers later that year — so Michaels went on&nbsp;
camera in September 1976 and pleaded with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:03.069
Beatles to reunite on his show and play three&nbsp;
songs for a comically small amount of money.

00:15:03.069 --> 00:15:06.933
"I was authorized by NBC to pay&nbsp;
them the sum of three thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.933 --> 00:15:11.160
dollars. That was three thousand&nbsp;
dollars for just three songs."

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:16.680
It was all a joke, but unbeknownst to Michaels&nbsp;
at the time, two Beatles actually considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:20.880
accepting. Paul McCartney and John Lennon&nbsp;
happened to be together watching "SNL" on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:24.780
TV at Lennon's apartment when Michaels made&nbsp;
his pitch. McCartney told Rock News Desk:

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:28.920
"John said, 'We should go down, just you and&nbsp;
me. There's only two of us so we'll take half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:33.236
the money.' But it would have been work, and we&nbsp;
were having a night off, so we elected not to go."

00:15:33.534 --> 00:15:38.940
Five years into "SNL," Michaels needed a break.&nbsp;
In case he departed entirely, he picked staff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:43.500
writer and cast member Al Franken to be his&nbsp;
replacement. Two days before a discussion with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.500 --> 00:15:48.660
NBC president Fred Silverman, Franken appeared in&nbsp;
a "Weekend Update" segment called "Limo for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:53.760
Lame-O," criticizing Silverman for charging NBC&nbsp;
for car service despite the network's failures.

00:15:53.760 --> 00:16:02.400
"The guy's been here two years, and hasn't&nbsp;
done diddly-squat. And he gets a limo. Now…"

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:05.700
That enraged Silverman and led to such animosity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:09.000
toward "SNL" that Michaels wound&nbsp;
up leaving the show altogether.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:15.180
NBC appointed inexperienced TV producer Jean&nbsp;
Doumanian to replace Michaels. Her first task:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:20.580
hire new performers, as most of the old ones left&nbsp;
with Michaels. Doumanian hired stars like Eddie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:25.680
Murphy and Joe Piscopo, while the show cycled&nbsp;
through writers. During the 1980-81 season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:30.300
"SNL" lost 30% of its audience while&nbsp;
critics lamented the slide in quality.

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:34.740
The 11th episode of the season featured&nbsp;
an extended riff on the "Who Shot J.R.?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:39.300
storyline from "Dallas," with cast member&nbsp;
Charles Rocket faux-shot. During the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:44.756
"goodnights," guest host Charlene Tilton asked&nbsp;
a wheelchair-bound Rocket how he felt. He said:

00:16:44.756 --> 00:16:47.160
"It's the first time I've ever been shot in&nbsp;
my life. I'd like to know who the f— did it."

00:16:49.501 --> 00:16:52.980
One episode later, NBC fired&nbsp;
Doumanian. Soon after that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:57.801
Rocket was let go as well. A writer's&nbsp;
strike followed, and the season ended early.

00:16:57.801 --> 00:17:03.420
"Saturday Night Live" is a high-profile pinnacle&nbsp;
of comedy, but it's not for everyone. Now best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:07.920
known for "Strangers with Candy" and "BoJack&nbsp;
Horseman," Amy Sedaris was performing her comic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:12.000
stage show "One Woman Shoe" in 1995 when&nbsp;
Michaels approached her about replacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:16.620
abruptly departed cast member Janeane&nbsp;
Garofalo. Sedaris told writer David Rakoff:

00:17:16.620 --> 00:17:20.340
"It was everything I wanted. Maybe even three&nbsp;
years earlier it would have been great."

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:25.260
Right around the time she landed a breakout role&nbsp;
on "Friends" in the mid-1990s, Jennifer Aniston&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:29.400
met with Michaels and turned down the chance&nbsp;
to be in the "SNL cast." She recalled telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:33.468
Michaels about the show's boys club atmosphere&nbsp;
during an appearance on "The Howard Stern Show":

00:17:33.468 --> 00:17:35.568
"I didn't think I would like that environment."

00:17:35.568 --> 00:17:39.600
Johnny Knoxville passed on "SNL" in&nbsp;
two different ways. Knoxville and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.900
team of skateboarders put together a&nbsp;
pilot of themselves performing wild,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:48.360
humorous pranks and stunts. That project aired&nbsp;
on MTV as "Jackass" after Knoxville turned down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:53.100
a weekly spot for the concept on "SNL," because&nbsp;
they'd have more control over the material on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:58.140
cable. "SNL" then asked Knoxville, alone, to&nbsp;
join the cast. He told the Washington Times:

00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:00.840
"It was at the point where I&nbsp;
either say yes to my friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:04.920
where we had all the control or yes to&nbsp;
'Saturday Night Live,' where none of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:08.301
my friends were really going to&nbsp;
be there and I had no control."

00:18:08.301 --> 00:18:13.106
"Saturday Night Live" writers are unionized&nbsp;
and part of the Writers Guild of America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.106 --> 00:18:17.040
When the organization goes on strike,&nbsp;
it ends production on most scripted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:20.940
television series. Two such stoppages&nbsp;
resulted in unique "Saturday Night Live"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:25.980
presentations made by "SNL" cast and writers&nbsp;
and presented live, but never aired on TV.

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:31.140
According to "The War for Late Night" book,&nbsp;
when the 1988 writers' strike caused "SNL" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:37.500
wrap up the 1987-88 season early, writers Robert&nbsp;
Smigel and Bob Odenkirk took all the sketches they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:42.420
didn't think would ever make it into a broadcast&nbsp;
episode of "SNL." After recruiting future writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:47.580
Conan O'Brien and Greg Daniels, the group put&nbsp;
on "The Happy Happy Good Show" at a theater in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:52.140
Chicago. Among the sketches in the presentation&nbsp;
was "Chicago Superfans," which would later be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:56.901
a recurring "SNL" bit called "Bill Swerski's&nbsp;
Superfans," responsible for the catchphrase:

00:18:56.901 --> 00:18:59.734
"Da Bears."
"Da Bears."

00:18:59.734 --> 00:19:05.640
During the 2007-2008 WGA strike, the Upright&nbsp;
Citizens Brigade Theater in New York hosted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:10.920
a complete "SNL" episode for a live audience.&nbsp;
With proceeds benefiting furloughed "SNL" staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:15.300
most of the show's regular cast at the&nbsp;
time — including Fred Armisen, Seth Meyers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:20.580
Darrell Hammond, Kenan Thompson, and Amy Poehler&nbsp;
— participated. Michael Cera served as guest host,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:24.960
and Yo La Tengo was the musical guest. Because&nbsp;
of the writers' strike, the sketches were written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:30.734
months or years earlier — all of them consisting&nbsp;
of things rejected for the TV version of "SNL."

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:35.280
It's not uncommon to meet a romantic partner&nbsp;
in the workplace, and "Saturday Night Live"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:39.360
seems to be the kind of place where people&nbsp;
make lasting love connections. "Star Wars"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:45.240
favorite Carrie Fisher hosted "SNL" in 1978 and&nbsp;
met Dan Aykroyd there, but according to Aykroyd,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:49.800
their romance began while filming "The Blues&nbsp;
Brothers." Scarlett Johansson hosted "SNL"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:53.700
in 2006 when Colin Jost was a writer on the&nbsp;
show, and they worked on a sketch together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.700 --> 00:20:00.000
in 2010 when Johansson returned. By 2017, they&nbsp;
were an official couple; they married in 2020.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.940
"And I met the love of my&nbsp;
life here. Merry Christmas."

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:09.720
Writer-director Dave McCary helmed&nbsp;
the "SNL" short "Wells for Boys"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:12.960
during an episode hosted by Emma&nbsp;
Stone, whom he'd later marry.

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:18.120
When "Mad Men" star Jon Hamm hosted "SNL" in&nbsp;
2008, he brought along costar Elisabeth Moss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:22.740
for a cameo in a sketch, where she met fellow&nbsp;
cast member Fred Armisen — the pair married in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:28.800
2009 and split up in 2010. Cast member Cecily&nbsp;
Strong dated featured player and writer Mike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:34.320
O'Brien in 2014. Pete Davidson met comedian&nbsp;
Cazzie David when her father, Larry David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:40.200
hosted the show in 2015. Davidson broke things&nbsp;
off in 2018, and days later began a relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:45.840
with Ariana Grande, whom he'd first met when&nbsp;
she appeared on "SNL" in 2016. Davidson later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:51.301
enjoyed a romance with Kim Kardashian, whom he&nbsp;
connected with after her 2021 hosting stint.

00:20:51.301 --> 00:20:56.280
Alongside the ever-changing cast of actors&nbsp;
and comedians on "Saturday Night Live" each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.280 --> 00:21:01.200
week is the show's house band. A collective of&nbsp;
experienced, professional musicians spanning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:06.120
many genres, a number of major stars have spent&nbsp;
some time playing the "SNL" opening and closing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:10.920
themes, jazzy-pop interstitial tunes, and&nbsp;
sketch accompaniment music as required.

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:15.300
Howard Shore, who'd later go on to win Oscars&nbsp;
for two "The Lord of the Rings" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.300 --> 00:21:19.260
wrote the "SNL" theme song and served&nbsp;
as the show's first musical director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:25.200
following collaborations with Lorne Michaels&nbsp;
on Canadian variety TV shows. The 1972 Toronto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:30.000
production of the musical "Godspell" involved&nbsp;
future "SNL" greats Gilda Radner and Martin Short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:35.940
while Paul Shaffer played in the orchestra. Before&nbsp;
joining the "SNL" cast as an actor — and before a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:40.860
33-year stint as David Letterman's bandleader&nbsp;
— Shaffer played keyboards in the "SNL" band.

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:46.320
Guitarist G.E. Smith became moderately famous as&nbsp;
a member of Daryl Hall and John Oates' backing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:50.520
band during their hit-making days in the&nbsp;
1980s, but needed work when the duo called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:56.460
for an indefinite hiatus in 1985. That's when&nbsp;
Michaels returned to "SNL" after his own hiatus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.460 --> 00:22:00.369
and according to The Morning Call, he&nbsp;
asked Smith to be the show's bandleader.

00:22:00.369 --> 00:22:05.580
Still photography comprises a big part of both&nbsp;
the look and flow of "Saturday Night Live."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:10.740
In transitioning to and from commercial breaks,&nbsp;
"SNL" puts up photos of each episode's guest host,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.740 --> 00:22:16.500
generally of a humorous and whimsical nature.&nbsp;
Since 1999, "SNL" official photographer Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:20.580
Ellen Matthews is the person responsible&nbsp;
for those "bumper" celebrity portraits.

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:22.560
Matthews takes the job seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.560 --> 00:22:27.240
aiming to make the host comfortable enough to&nbsp;
let loose and be creative. She told Vulture:

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:31.080
"I like to make it as easy as possible for&nbsp;
everyone. I don't want them overthinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:36.120
this part of the show. It should be super fun and&nbsp;
super easy. It's an open invitation to get kooky."

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:40.560
The "SNL" production schedule is so tightly&nbsp;
packed that Matthews and a handful of costume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:44.520
and prop assistants get no more than&nbsp;
90 minutes over the span of two days to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:48.840
shoot an entire episode's bumpers, about&nbsp;
four or five different visual scenarios,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:51.840
which makes proper planning key. Matthews added:

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:54.660
"We try to find out who's&nbsp;
hosting as far in advance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:57.115
as possible so we can start thinking about it."

00:22:57.244 --> 00:23:00.480
In 2021, Lorne Michaels told reporters that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:03.445
might be winding down work&nbsp;
on the comedy institution.

00:23:03.445 --> 00:23:10.740
"My plan is to be here for the 50th. And by that&nbsp;
point, I really deserve to, uh, wander off."

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:13.020
Michaels doesn't want "SNL" to end with his exit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:16.500
however, and he claims to have&nbsp;
secretly selected his successor.

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:19.800
Less than a year later, Charlamagne&nbsp;
tha God reported on his show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:24.120
"Hell of a Week," a rumor that NBC may&nbsp;
pull the plug on "SNL" when Michaels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:28.920
leaves after Season 50. "Hell of a Week"&nbsp;
guest Kenan Thompson — the longest-tenured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:32.456
cast member in the history of the&nbsp;
show — didn't deny the gossip.

00:23:32.456 --> 00:23:35.700
"There could be a lot of validity&nbsp;
to that rumor because 50 is a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:37.020
number to stop at, ya know what I'm saying?"

